<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
    Drops eleven secondary indexes that cannot serve a query, measured on a copy of the production
    database (2026-08-03 snapshot). Together they occupy 1.80 GB. On result, participation and
    build_job the secondary indexes currently outweigh the rows they point at - 2,324 MB against
    1,177 MB on result, 791 MB against 345 MB on participation, 721 MB against 631 MB on build_job.

    Sizes below are page counts from mysql.innodb_index_stats after ANALYZE, taken on a freshly
    rebuilt schema so they are live pages rather than fragmentation.

    Group 1 - a leftmost prefix of a composite index that already exists (717 MB)

      Each of these indexes a single column that is already the first column of a wider index.
      Verified with EXPLAIN on the production copy: with the narrow index ignored, every lookup
      falls through to the composite as the same "Covering index lookup", and the estimated cost
      barely moves (0.53 -> 0.91 in the worst of the four).

        submission.FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq (participation_id)            306 MB
          -> idx_submission_participation_submission_date (participation_id, submission_date DESC)
        result.fk_result_submission_id (submission_id)                       297 MB
          -> idx_result_submission_completion_date (submission_id, completion_date DESC)
        participation.fk_participation_student_id (student_id)                68 MB
          -> UK6skuc03gvp2gw5lyji5lu0v0v (student_id, exercise_id, initialization_state, attempt)
        participation.fk_participation_team_id (team_id)                      46 MB
          -> UC_PARTICIPATION_TEAM_ID_EXERCISE_ID_INITIALIZATION_STATE_COLS (team_id, exercise_id, initialization_state)

      All four back a foreign key, and in each case the index carries the same name as the
      constraint. MySQL requires an index for a foreign key but accepts any index that has the
      referencing column leftmost, so these drops succeed and the constraint survives; this was
      confirmed against MySQL directly, including the case where the composite's second column is
      DESC, and the control case (a foreign key whose only index is the one being dropped) fails
      with ERROR 1553 as expected. PostgreSQL never requires an index for a foreign key.

    Group 2 - too few distinct values to be selective (723 MB)

        result.result_rated (rated)                                          215 MB   2 distinct values
        submission.jhi_type (jhi_type)                                       212 MB   6 distinct values
        submission.submission_discriminator (discriminator)                  198 MB   4 distinct values
        build_job.idx_build_job_result (build_job_result)                     53 MB   4 distinct values
        participation.participation_discriminator (discriminator)             45 MB   3 distinct values

      Note that build_job.idx_build_job_result indexes the build_job_result status column. It is not
      idx_build_job_result_id, which indexes result_id and backs the fk_build_job_result constraint;
      that index is kept.

      Every query that filters on these columns also filters on a selective one - exercise_id,
      participation_id or submission_id - and EXPLAIN on the production copy shows the optimizer
      choosing that index and applying these columns as a post-filter.

    Group 3 - indexed but never used as a predicate (45 MB)

        participation.participation_presentation_score (presentation_score)   45 MB

      presentationScore appears only in JPQL select lists, never in a WHERE or JOIN clause. The
      index also covers 2,775,708 rows to describe the 10,113 that are non-null.

    Group 4 - selective in principle, unused in practice (340 MB)

        result.result_assessment_type (assessment_type)                      340 MB

      This one is listed separately because the usual cardinality argument does not settle it:
      assessment_type has four values but MANUAL covers only 2.5% of rows (312,724 of 12.5M), which
      is selective enough for the optimizer to pick the index - and it does, for a query that
      filters on assessment_type alone and selects only indexed columns.

      No such query exists in Artemis. Every JPQL query that mentions assessmentType pairs it with a
      selective driver (p.exercise.id, result.submission.id IN ...) or with a date range that has its
      own index, and each of them projects columns the index does not carry. EXPLAIN on the
      production copy confirms the optimizer chooses idx_result_exercise_id, fk_result_submission_id
      or result_completion_date for those shapes and never result_assessment_type. If a reviewer
      prefers to confirm this against production query plans first, this changeset can be removed
      without touching the other ten.

    All eleven indexes are created by 00000000000000_initial_schema.xml, which cannot be edited
    without invalidating recorded checksums, so - as with changelog 20260816213553 - fresh
    installations create them and drop them again here, which costs milliseconds on empty tables.
    Each changeset is a single DDL statement guarded by an indexExists precondition, so a schema that
    has already lost an index (or never had it, since index names can differ between the two
    databases) marks the changeset as run instead of failing startup.

    DROP INDEX is an INPLACE metadata-only operation on MySQL 8.0+ and on PostgreSQL, so this
    migration is instant regardless of table size. The freed pages return to the tablespace
    immediately; the files themselves shrink only on the next table rebuild.

    Rollback recreates each index with its original name and column, so reverting to the previous
    WAR finds a schema it can read and write either way - nothing here is dropped that the previous
    version writes, since an index is not part of any INSERT or UPDATE statement.
    -->

    <changeSet id="20260916211144-01-drop-submission-participation-fk-index" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <indexExists tableName="submission" indexName="FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq"/>
        </preConditions>
        <dropIndex tableName="submission" indexName="FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq"/>
        <rollback>
            <createIndex tableName="submission" indexName="FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq">
                <column name="participation_id"/>
            </createIndex>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

